WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.534
359,000 Calls: How Lobbyists Bought Canadian Policy.

00:00:04.534 --> 00:00:08.125
A forensic examination of Canada's federal lobbying registry.

00:00:08.125 --> 00:00:09.772
Every communication logged.

00:00:09.772 --> 00:00:11.772
Every policy outcome correlated.

00:00:11.772 --> 00:00:15.579
Every dollar traced through public records that your government hoped you'd never read.

00:00:15.579 --> 00:00:16.965
The lobbying machine.

00:00:16.965 --> 00:00:20.375
Over 9,000 active lobbyists are registered federally.

00:00:20.375 --> 00:00:25.636
Canada's federal lobbying system is vast, legal, and meticulously documented.

00:00:25.636 --> 00:00:36.193
The Office of the Commissioner of Lobbying (OCL) requires every individual paid to communicate with federal officials on behalf of a client or employer to register and report their activities.

00:00:36.193 --> 00:00:37.465
Sector breakdown.

00:00:37.465 --> 00:00:38.965
Who's lobbying whom.

00:00:38.965 --> 00:00:45.579
From pharmaceuticals to defence to telecommunications to finance — which industries spend the most and which departments they target.

00:00:45.579 --> 00:00:47.409
The pharma to MAID pipeline.

00:00:47.409 --> 00:00:55.477
How pharmaceutical industry lobbying intersected with the expansion of medical assistance in dying — from eligibility criteria to mental health provisions.

00:00:55.477 --> 00:00:57.636
Defence contractors to procurement.

00:00:57.636 --> 00:01:04.954
How defence industry lobbying shapes military procurement decisions — from the fighter jet competition to naval shipbuilding to armoured vehicles.

00:01:04.954 --> 00:01:07.602
Tech sector to content regulation bills.

00:01:07.602 --> 00:01:17.306
How Silicon Valley giants lobbied Ottawa on Bills C-11, C-18, and the Online Harms Act — shaping the laws that regulate their own platforms.

00:01:17.306 --> 00:01:18.545
The revolving door.

00:01:18.545 --> 00:01:27.090
Former ministers, chiefs of staff, and senior officials who registered as lobbyists within months of leaving government — selling access to their former colleagues.

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:29.693
Elections Canada contribution overlap.

00:01:29.693 --> 00:01:37.181
Political donations by registered lobbyists and their firms — mapping the financial relationship between lobbying clients and party fundraising.
